WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.880
If the dance scene in Netflix's&nbsp;
"Wednesday" is so awesome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.580
why are people so upset&nbsp;
about it? Let's take a look.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.840
"Wednesday," is definitely off to a good&nbsp;
start on the streaming giant. The "Addams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.620
Family" spinoff exploded on the platform,&nbsp;
recording the most hours viewed in a week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.940
for an English-language series. Jenna&nbsp;
Ortega is absolutely the standout of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.780
"Wednesday," as everyone has been praising&nbsp;
her performance. Unfortunately for her though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:25.560
newly-surfaced information has her&nbsp;
trending online for all the wrong reasons.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.960
"They want answers, and so do I!"

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.320
"I would lead the inquisition, but I&nbsp;
left my thumb screws and rack at home."

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.400
During a recent interview with NME, the&nbsp;
actress unintentionally threw herself under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.060
the bus. As the interview dives deeper&nbsp;
into the show, Wednesday's dance number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.220
found in Episode 4, "Woe What a Night," became&nbsp;
the topic of conversation. Ortega revealed:

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:59.340
The interviewer was confused by the statement,&nbsp;
which led Ortega to more or less double down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.420
on it. She reflected on waking up with body&nbsp;
aches and a sore throat and still went to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.740
set and filmed the scene, receiving medicine&nbsp;
in between takes. She kept filming until her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.000
test results came back positive. MGM later&nbsp;
told the outlet that the studio implemented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.560
strict COVID protocols after the tests confirmed&nbsp;
Ortega had COVID, but fans weren't having it.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.460
While NME released the interview nearly&nbsp;
a month ago, the information surrounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.960
Ortega's COVID diagnosis recently took&nbsp;
off on social media. It doesn't help the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.500
situation that the interview formatted the&nbsp;
story in a way that showed Ortega's strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:32.340
in pushing through the performance while&nbsp;
battling the illness. Regardless of intent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:35.340
fans didn't see it that way at&nbsp;
all. One twitter user stated:

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:49.380
Many agreed with the user's sentiment,&nbsp;
echoing their disappointment for Ortega,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.200
director Tim Burton, and Netflix&nbsp;
for putting more importance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.380
the scene than the lives of the&nbsp;
cast and crew. Another user added:

00:02:06.646 --> 00:02:09.240
Most likely, this is much bigger than Ortega.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:13.680
Another Twitter user put into question&nbsp;
the COVID protocols on set. Since Ortega&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.860
woke up with symptoms, any protocols that&nbsp;
were actually in place should have kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:20.880
her isolated until the test results returned.&nbsp;
And another user brought things full circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.880
saying that these events are the&nbsp;
result of American culture that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.060
glorifies someone risking their life&nbsp;
and the lives of others for their job.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:31.680
Some Twitter users came to Ortega's defense,&nbsp;
saying that other people are at fault for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.400
not having the actress isolate while she&nbsp;
waited for her results. One user wrote:

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.820
Other users took it upon themselves to&nbsp;
blame Netflix. No one can be sure what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.600
went on behind the scenes, but there was&nbsp;
plenty of theorizing in the comments. Some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.660
suggested it was just routine testing&nbsp;
and it happened to come out positive.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:02.040
These events aren't even the first time that the&nbsp;
production of "Wednesday" came under fire. Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.857
week, fans took to social media to criticize&nbsp;
the show's portrayal of Black characters.

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:07.440
"Where has she hithered?"

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.053
"You mean Wednesday Addams? Knowing her&nbsp;
she's hithered somewhere she shan't be."

00:03:13.053 --> 00:03:17.220
As the director of the first four episodes, Burton&nbsp;
was the target of criticism for using the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:21.420
black characters as the main antagonists,&nbsp;
with many fans claiming it was a racist move.

